{
  "index": 1,
  "response": " In the 1620s, the Spanish colony in Lima sent 60,000 pesos every two months to pay for the labor of 3,000 to 4,000 Indians working in the mercury mine at Huanacavelica. The work conditions were harsh as miners had to break up ore, fill sacks, and climb ladders under poor lighting and loud noise. The superintendents were undisciplined and thievish, exacerbating the workers' suffering.\n\nIn northeast Siberia during the 1740s, the local Yakut and Tungus natives were forced to pay a tribute known as yasak. Initially, they paid with furs such as sables or red foxes, but due to overhunting, these animals have become scarce. As a result, many natives cannot hunt because they no longer have horses, which had been pawned to yasak collectors.",
